Title: "First-Order Alphabets, Terms, and Formulas"
Id: "fd6bdfe1-8777-45ac-abef-492fa5a6ffa3"


Text: "While propositional logic provides the governing principles for composing
       indivisible assertions, it cannot analyze their internal structure.
       Mathematical assertions such as 'for every real number x > 0, there
       exists y such that y^2 = x' require decomposing assertions into objects,
       functions, relations, and quantifiers.

       In 1879, Gottlob Frege introduced the foundational architecture of
       first-order predicate logic, replacing the Aristotelian subject-predicate
       grammar of classical syllogisms with mathematical functions, arguments,
       and quantifiers. First-order syntax separates expressions into two
       fundamental tiers: terms, which denote objects in a domain, and formulas,
       which make assertions concerning those objects."
Id: "f693c2ec-7a4c-4d9b-baf7-ae51116b2238"


[\fol.variable]
Declares: x
Documented:
. called: "first-order individual variable"
. written: "x"
. description: "A formal variable symbol representing an unspecified element of
                the domain of discourse."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{67}
Id: "24f9ca50-7f34-4a9f-8314-e10e92fb9e3b"


[\fol.term]
Declares: t
Documented:
. called: "first-order term"
. written: "t"
. description: "A syntactically valid expression representing an object,
                generated inductively from individual variables, constants, and
                function applications."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{68}
Id: "ef278f0b-7cdf-48be-ab28-e95ed37d77fc"


[\fol.constant]
Declares: c is \fol.term
Documented:
. called: "individual constant symbol"
. written: "c"
. description: "A 0-ary non-logical symbol designating a specific fixed element
                of the universe of discourse."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{68}
Id: "5c8d7bf5-96c9-4ef7-9abe-582719704c05"


[\fol.function.symbol{n}]
Defines: f is \function
when: n is \natural
Documented:
. called: "n-ary function symbol"
. written: "f"
. description: "An n-ary non-logical function symbol representing a mapping from
                n-tuples of domain objects to domain objects."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{68}
Id: "ae0b4305-25ce-4762-8077-2aa9011bfb31"


[\fol.predicate.symbol{n}]
Defines: P is \relation
when: n is \natural
Documented:
. called: "n-ary predicate symbol"
. written: "P"
. description: "An n-ary non-logical relation symbol representing an n-place
                condition or relation on the domain of discourse."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{68}
Id: "65ee7c1a-423b-4566-90df-2ed0b6152613"


Text: "Terms designate objects; to assert relationships between objects, we
       combine terms with predicate symbols or the equality relation. The
       resulting expressions are atomic formulas, which serve as the fundamental
       indivisible statements of first-order logic."
Id: "158fc1f1-9e11-4942-a81f-413c1fe1fbe5"


[\fol.atomic.formula]
Declares: A is \\statement
Documented:
. called: "atomic first-order formula"
. written: "A"
. description: "An atomic assertion formed either by the equality of two terms
                (t_1 = t_2) or by an n-ary predicate symbol applied to an
                n-tuple of terms P(t_1, ..., t_n)."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{69}
Id: "bcefeed4-b24c-4360-baff-726ed0c200f3"


Text: "Compound formulas are built inductively from atomic formulas using the
       standard propositional connectives (negation, conjunction, disjunction,
       implication, equivalence) together with universal (\forall) and
       existential (\exists) quantification over individual variables."
Id: "9bf821ac-da6b-4c9f-b157-6753e59ab766"


[\fol.formula]
Declares: phi is \\statement
Documented:
. called: "well-formed first-order formula"
. written: "\varphi"
. description: "A grammatically valid first-order formula generated inductively
                from atomic formulas via propositional connectives and
                quantifiers."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{69}
Id: "f3a79b3e-557a-4ae5-a297-8992961a0b25"


[\fol.for.all{x}:in{D}{P}]
Defines: A is \\statement
when:
. x is \fol.variable
. D is \set
. P is \\statement
Documented:
. called: "universal quantification $\forall x \in D, P$"
. written: "\forall x \in D, P"
. description: "The assertion that condition P holds for every element x in
                domain D."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{70}
Id: "b9365ecb-30f9-42f8-a988-4405d7a8dcd2"


[\fol.exists{x}:in{D}{P}]
Defines: A is \\statement
when:
. x is \fol.variable
. D is \set
. P is \\statement
Documented:
. called: "existential quantification $\exists x \in D, P$"
. written: "\exists x \in D, P"
. description: "The assertion that condition P holds for at least one element x
                in domain D."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{70}
Id: "362f5cff-4461-4466-9fce-06a26a84fa45"


Text: "In a quantified formula, the quantifier introduces a scope over a
       variable. An occurrence of a variable x is bound if it lies within the
       scope of a quantifier binding x; otherwise, the occurrence is free. A
       variable may occur both freely and boundly in different subformulas of
       the same statement."
Id: "58440b62-ef99-4c2f-8718-85350b1ca225"


[\fol.free.variables{phi}]
Defines: V is \set
when: phi is \\statement
Documented:
. called: "set of free variables $\operatorname{FV}(\varphi)$"
. written: "\operatorname{FV}(\varphi?)"
. description: "The finite set of individual variables having at least one free
                occurrence in formula \varphi."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{75}
Id: "9ef664a1-0491-4232-92a5-cea2eb417736"


Text: "A formula containing free variables expresses a property or open
       condition whose truth depends on the values assigned to those variables.
       When a formula contains no free variables whatsoever, it is called a
       sentence. Sentences represent self-contained declarative mathematical
       assertions whose truth value depends exclusively on the chosen
       interpretation structure."
Id: "48aa4a07-2723-444d-ba57-735c4e848f83"


[\fol.sentence]
Declares: sigma is \\statement
satisfies:
. \fol.free.variables{sigma} = \empty.set
Documented:
. called: "first-order sentence"
. written: "\sigma"
. description: "A closed first-order formula possessing no free variable
                occurrences."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{75}
Id: "6bb9bf01-0b9a-4e77-8313-71ce58314919"


Text: "The syntactic operation of substituting a term t for free occurrences of
       a variable x in a formula \varphi, denoted \varphi[t/x], must be executed
       with utmost care. If the term t contains a variable y that happens to
       fall within the scope of a quantifier binding y in \varphi, accidental
       variable capture occurs, distorting the intended logical meaning. A term
       t is substitutable for x in \varphi (free for x in \varphi) when no
       variable in t becomes bound as a consequence of the substitution."
Id: "798bd682-8c2d-4f27-9642-2ad0f1389140"


[\fol.substitution{t}:for{x}:in{phi}]
Defines: psi is \\statement
when:
. t is \fol.term
. x is \fol.variable
. phi is \\statement
Documented:
. called: "substitution $\varphi[t/x]$"
. written: "\varphi?[t?/x?]"
. description: "The formula obtained by replacing every free occurrence of
                variable x in \varphi with term t, renaming bound variables as
                necessary to avoid capture."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{112}
Id: "2cf6b23a-d67d-4c08-8f64-ff0053d5ff71"


Theorem:
given:
. phi is \\statement
. x is \fol.variable
. t is \fol.term
then:
. (\fol.substitution{t}:for{x}:in{phi} is \\statement)
Documented:
. called: "syntactic closure of capture-free substitution"
. description: "For any well-formed formula \varphi, variable x, and term t, the
                capture-free substitution \varphi[t/x] produces a unique,
                well-formed first-order formula."
References:
. $enderton.logic:page{112}
Id: "450ebf1a-df9c-490f-bc21-a785cd09bda4"
